  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/tetranematichthys_wallacei_en/",
      "html": "<p>Maximum total length about 20 cm. Carnivorous. Origin: Venezuela.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/tetranematichthys_wallacei_en/",
      "html": "<p>Three different species of the genus Tetranematichthys are currently distinguished by scientists. None of them is often imported, because their secretive way of life between dead driftwood makes catching them a pure matter of luck. This is the first importation ever of Tetranematichthys wallacei, from Venezuela. Three specimens were obtained: one male and two females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/tetranematichthys_wallacei_en/",
      "html": "<p>A tank for Tetranematichthys should contain fine sand on the bottom, be softly lit and be equipped with a great number of pieces of driftwood.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/tetranematichthys_wallacei_en/",
      "html": "<p>It is carnivorous and prefers to feed on small fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/tetranematichthys_wallacei_en/",
      "html": "<p>During the daytime the fish often sleep lying on their side. This behaviour is absolutely normal and no reason for concern. Towards conspecifics Tetranematichthys are completely peaceful.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/tetranematichthys_wallacei_en/",
      "html": "<p>As in the closely related genus Ageinosus, males of Tetranematichthys develop enormous dorsal fins during the breeding season. The spine of that fin is very thorny. During mating the males attach to the female with this thorny spine. Fertilisation is internal, by a penis-like organ formed from modified rays of the anal fin. It is said that after the breeding season the male's dorsal fin reduces to the size of the female's.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/tetranematichthys_wallacei_en/",
      "html": "<p>Tetranematichthys means \"fish with four threads\", referring to the barbels. wallacei is a dedication name for Alfred Russel Wallace (1823-1913).</p>"
    }
  ],
